fine, and when they are not obvious, you can mark them with pe'a.

> In the Netherlands and Russia they say "It's Chinese to me" in such situa=
tions.

In Germany, people say "das kommt mir spanisch vor" =3D "this seems spanish=
 to me" to exress not-understanding or that something is odd. Using foreign=
 languages in such metaphors is common all over the planet, so using .itkui=
l (or any other non-lojban language, even though the comical effect is grea=
ter with .itkuil, it being another conlang and known for its complexity) wi=
ll do the job. Again, use pe'a or zo'o when you aren't sure people will get=
 it.

